  "transcript": "# Delivering East West Rail\n\n## Why no passenger services yet\n> Most infrastructure and trains are ready, but services are blocked mainly by an unresolved staffing dispute.\n\nChair: The line between Oxford and Milton Keynes opened 744 days ago — why has no passenger service yet run?\nEddie Dempsey: There were technical delays and a late contract award after the election, but for us the key issue is staffing: formal consultation on staff arrangements only started in July, and we're still negotiating what the second person on board will do.\nJodie Lofthouse: I share residents' frustration. The Department and Chiltern have completed much of the mobilisation: driver training, train modifications and station work, but we still need an agreed operating model before we set a service date.\nTony Baxter: Chiltern has done the train modifications, recruited and trained drivers, and largely finished station fit-out at Winslow — the remaining blocking point is reaching agreement with the unions on the method of operation.\n\n\n## The unions' safety argument\n> RMT wants a second safety‑critical staff member with full guard responsibilities; the operator proposes CSIs with limited safety duties — the disagreement is small in pay but central to safety and roles.\n\nChair: RMT, is this dispute primarily about passenger safety or about jobs and timing?\nEddie Dempsey: It's about passenger safety. We want a second safety‑critical person able to carry out full guard duties: evacuation, protecting the line and managing the platform‑train interface.\nAlex Mayer: How many staff are we talking about and what's the pay difference?\nEddie Dempsey: Fewer than 40 people. The difference in pay for a second safety‑critical role is about £5,000 each; the total cost to resolve it is roughly £150k–£200k a year.\nAlex Mayer: Is dispatch the main safety concern — why can't the driver handle doors and dispatch?\nEddie Dempsey: Drivers can operate doors, but the platform‑train interface is where many injuries occur; having another trained person to manage dispatch, accessibility and incidents gives extra assurance and can prevent traps and drags.\nSteff Aquarone: The ORR considers DOO safe in many cases — why do you disagree with the regulator?\nEddie Dempsey: My experience as a driver and safety rep tells me having staff on board who can carry out full safety duties matters, especially at night and for vulnerable passengers; this is as much about the kind of railway we want as about formal regulation.\nDave Calfe: ASLEF is waiting for the RMT to conclude its talks; we will then discuss driver arrangements. We support a properly crewed railway and aren't rushing into a separate dispute.\n\n\n## Operational readiness and other issues\n> Train and station work is almost done; outstanding items are union agreement, final training, safety validations and regulatory approvals.\n\nTony Baxter: We've taken on the 196 trains, completed modifications, recruited 44 drivers and finished most station fit‑outs; Winslow handover is in progress and some legal lease issues are now resolved.\nAlex Mayer: How long after an agreement would it take to start services?\nTony Baxter: It's difficult to give a firm date because some final steps need partners' input, but the remaining tasks — updating training, final driver route and train training, and safety validation with the ORR — are routine.\nJodie Lofthouse: The Department supported appointing Chiltern as operator; risks like industrial relations were on our registers and we set escalation routes when they materialised.\nDaniel Francis: Does public ownership of Chiltern from September 2026 change the prospects?\nTony Baxter: Not materially. Industrial relations are generally good; being publicly owned doesn't remove the need for approvals and agreements, but we continue to work closely with the Department.\nDave Calfe: One practical lesson: use existing depots. Drivers were initially based at Banbury but work was moved to Bletchley — using the existing depot at Bletchley would have been more efficient.\n\n\n## Lessons learned and next steps\n> Officials say key lessons are earlier operator involvement and integrated delivery; all parties say they want a quick resolution and expect weeks rather than years, but no firm start date was given.\n\nJodie Lofthouse: Key lessons for future phases: bring the operator in earlier, adopt a systems view with all delivery partners, and give the delivery body a strong integration role to avoid late changes.\nTony Baxter: Early operating involvement avoids small late changes that cumulatively delay delivery — that includes station fit‑outs and detail design for services.\nOlly Glover: What will each of you do next to get the dispute resolved and services running, and when do you think that will happen?\nDave Calfe: ASLEF will engage fully once RMT and Chiltern conclude discussions. I can't give a precise timescale because we're waiting on those talks.\nTony Baxter: Chiltern is focused on negotiations, will keep liaising with the Department and will speed through final ratifications once there is agreement; we don't expect it to be years.\nJodie Lofthouse: The Department will continue to advise Ministers and support Chiltern; it's for Ministers to decide any further action but our objective is to get services running quickly.\nEddie Dempsey: We're in good faith negotiations and hopeful common sense will prevail; we think this could be wrapped up in weeks.\nChair: Thank you all. The meeting was illuminating — please follow up in writing if you want to add anything. We'll suspend briefly before the next panel.\n",
